{
  "language": "javascript",
  "case": "sequelize_exports",
  "why": "One shape per rule `settle` reads an imported model by (js-review-followup D5, D8), each model named differently from the binding that exports it, so no rule can hide behind a match on the model's name. A class exported under another name (`{ Performer: Soloist }`); every link of `exports.Stanza = exports.Couplet = define(\u2026)`; an ESM `export const` and `export { Noel as Carol }`; a whole-module import of a module whose default is one of its two models; a names-known map whose value this pass cannot name (`Object.assign(module.exports, { Rondo: m.rondo })`), which falls back to the model's name. Refused: a member of a member (`exports.api.Lyric`), a destructured `{ Aria }` of a module whose default *is* the model, a define written after `module.exports` was replaced, a top-level binding a hidden surface does not export, a case-aside match with two candidates, and a self-association only resolution reveals. `seed.js` holds a `define` whose `{ type: 'string' }` is an option, not a Sequelize type. The second review round added a shape per fix, each able to fail: a define written onto a *function's* `db` that shadows the exported one; members written onto an object `module.exports` later replaced, by `module.exports.X` and by an earlier literal; a model default that is also written as a member of itself (`module.exports.Opera = Opera`); a class model declared inside a function; a literal at the end of a *declared* chain; a frozen literal and a top-level `this.X`; an import naming no export of a readable module whose unexported model shares its name; a whole-module import of a module with two models and no default; and, in the importing file, a function-local define spelled like an import. The third round's shapes: a literal assigned onto a *parameter* named `module` or `exports`; `var` declared twice, where the later declaration wins; a `var` inside a top-level `if` and a top-level `try`, which belongs to the module, not the block; an ESM `export class` model; a names-known name with no nameable value beside a model default; and an ES module's top-level `this`. Two more, from the third: a function written *between* two `var` declarations, whose body reads the last one; a file that declares its own `var module`; a function invoked on the spot and a `static {}` block, each between two `var` declarations, which read the earlier one; and a class model default beside a names-known name with no nameable value.",
